WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:04.570
Learning to sail gave me new skills,

2
00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.050
but it didn't give me another pair of hands.

3
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:10.630
Hi, I'm Ryan and I created Auto Tiller.

4
00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:16.750
I often sailed without a teammate as I progressed to use a jib and a spinnaker,

5
00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:19.810
steering while adjusting the sails remained difficult.

6
00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:26.970
Auto Tiller is an app connected steering prototype designed to help solo sailors maintain a selected

7
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.870
heading while they manage the sails.

8
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:33.050
I built the mobile app with Dart and Flutter.

9
00:00:33.710 --> 00:00:36.810
It connects over Bluetooth to an ESP32 controller.

10
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:41.790
The system uses a 9-axis IMU to help estimate the boat's heading,

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:43.970
GPS for position information,

12
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:47.930
and a battery-powered linear actuator to move the tiller.

13
00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:56.330
In the app, I selected a target heading and engaged automatic steering.

14
00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:03.150
The controller compares that target with the measured heading and uses PID control to calculate

15
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:04.130
a steering correction.

16
00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.730
Here, I'm demonstrating the system on a bench.

17
00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:12.230
I rotate the sensor housing by hand to simulate the boat changing direction.

18
00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:16.970
You can see the actuator respond as the measured heading changes.

19
00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:22.130
This lets me examine the system's response before testing it on the water,

20
00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.650
where wind and waves will introduce additional challenges.

21
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:31.790
This screen provides a visual representation of the heading and steering position.

22
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:37.030
The GPS interface shows a position marker and lets me select a destination.

23
00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.470
This demonstrates the interface.

24
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:43.450
It does not establish the boat can navigate there autonomously.

25
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.980
Programming VEX Autonomous Routines gave me a foundation in feedback control,

26
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:51.280
but Auto Tiller brought new challenges,

27
00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:56.840
an actuator that wouldn't move turned out to have insufficient power from a faulty converter.

28
00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.980
PID tuning also required balancing quick corrections with smoother behaviors.

29
00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.500
AI helped with the initial framework and debugging,

30
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.280
I also wrote and modified the code and worked on testing and tuning.

31
00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.070
For sailors like me who don't always have a teammate,

32
00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.990
I want to make managing their sails and tiller less overwhelming.

33
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:23.310
My goal is practical steering assistance for small sailboats with sailor remaining in control.

34
00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:28.210
The next step is controlled on water testing and feedback from other sailors.

35
00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:32.110
Auto Tiller started with something I wished for on the water.

36
00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:34.510
This project is my attempt to build it.